From a chemical perspective, the high calcium oxide content in CCFA hydrates to form calcium hydroxide, which subsequently reacts with the amorphous aluminosilicates (SiO 2  and Al 2 O 3 ) present in MK. This pozzolanic reaction leads to the formation of calcium silicate hydrate (C–S–H), calcium aluminate hydrate (C–A–H), and calcium aluminosilicate hydrate (C–A–S–H) phases. The key reactions are summarised as follows [ 44 ,  45 ]: 1 Al 2 O 3 · 2 SiO 2 + xCa(OH) 2 + H 2 O → C-S-H + C-A-S-H + C 2 ASH 8 + C 4 AH 13 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
